#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8459" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039b1404-c3c5-5f0d-9a9a-7558551fd64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280986} {:answer/id #uuid "c34dde87-5094-5eee-91dc-6aa1b724f521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280987} {:answer/id #uuid "f6231a12-ab5e-5bbd-b99c-a535dd85e364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280988} {:answer/id #uuid "2adaaddc-1b23-5ecc-bb68-50ad35a9cc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280989} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5b2501-b5d5-505e-b2bc-8081f1f55e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2adaaddc-1b23-5ecc-bb68-50ad35a9cc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280989} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac17ce1a-7905-54b1-913b-6084706ce9ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a933fd6d-6a35-5eb0-9d87-5a41bdcc4a21" :question/math-academy-id "q-118779" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={x}^{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ which has order $6.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081ddc59-8da5-572e-a067-ebfe7fee2456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310188} {:answer/id #uuid "31828460-6eba-5650-8722-c0e1db601224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310189} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fc8b29-5ef2-58c0-807d-54dae19b8ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310190} {:answer/id #uuid "ec559370-184b-5d00-8c7f-d07ff6788d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310191} {:answer/id #uuid "2eced7cd-c016-56e3-a405-f3d575b9fbd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31828460-6eba-5650-8722-c0e1db601224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310189} :answer-field/id #uuid "5708abd5-c553-57d5-b6da-58776b034e7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6548405c-a613-5075-b99b-e7774982d1db" :question/math-academy-id "q-118780" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}-9{x}^{7}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ which has order $8.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b25a9c-a983-580c-878e-4224b7548f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294498} {:answer/id #uuid "5a40dba0-67d5-58e4-8b61-71b97c14184d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294499} {:answer/id #uuid "d4384449-e97e-50b5-b015-95f2f8b5633b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294500} {:answer/id #uuid "9b46aa2b-5494-54c2-99b8-f73a9e9492ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294501} {:answer/id #uuid "232133c4-05bd-5487-b748-93227ab76cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a40dba0-67d5-58e4-8b61-71b97c14184d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294499} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e606b0d-c1e8-5e4e-ac94-039518580d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d3e1743-cd34-559d-bad0-d34093686fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118786" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\n- $8{y}^{′}-xy=3$\n- $3{y}^{″}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "819837bc-3c26-5982-be1c-aba248d960bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310195} {:answer/id #uuid "0847046b-ab2c-5c03-9d24-c78203613c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310196} {:answer/id #uuid "7aff5497-9742-5c3b-ba29-c7220a0e52c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310197} {:answer/id #uuid "ff03baea-fc93-5382-974b-f89e934de061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310198} {:answer/id #uuid "cb518d2b-76e8-5383-9317-12e231c1d909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb518d2b-76e8-5383-9317-12e231c1d909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310199} :answer-field/id #uuid "023a2699-f925-5c39-8ed3-1368ca4c51ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e81dead2-8026-5bbf-8274-50b6fe156c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118788" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70d61d2a-27c7-5a37-af06-55a203b16f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281014} {:answer/id #uuid "012f77a9-3aa3-5515-bd22-99961b8ad332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281015} {:answer/id #uuid "113ab132-b40f-5ab4-be6a-6c9ba0884b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281016} {:answer/id #uuid "e94997a8-0a2e-5b11-84a6-6d30752c74f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281017} {:answer/id #uuid "777be402-ac0f-53da-98ec-0b10ae621300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "012f77a9-3aa3-5515-bd22-99961b8ad332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281015} :answer-field/id #uuid "40012c1d-87cf-5109-9a23-6aca313e1115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90a18919-f815-5b77-9ac2-909415c66f7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118791" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6297545-0a2b-5a58-b39b-6e19a58e8907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310202} {:answer/id #uuid "1e72bb06-a683-5964-828a-717fa36dc134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310203} {:answer/id #uuid "2a43bcfb-0432-5d5b-b8c6-771e30808d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310204} {:answer/id #uuid "69775569-6737-5840-9f64-2fbbe353abc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310205} {:answer/id #uuid "a0461470-74e1-595b-87a8-6bf2f3b6e07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0461470-74e1-595b-87a8-6bf2f3b6e07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310206} :answer-field/id #uuid "41e36352-b8f1-5f96-8328-34f73f65a743" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fdef7eb-2d32-5a05-8afa-f9593109442c" :question/math-academy-id "q-118859" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80337228-cea6-52db-8a4d-930989bdcebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165817} {:answer/id #uuid "89a35259-0f01-5b1d-ae22-c594d8128669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165818} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1c9270-aa03-5e77-b8f2-084d75b8a444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165819} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8035ae-c8d2-5924-9442-ddd0e028b5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165820} {:answer/id #uuid "20f481cb-9794-521f-abde-9e8e3e1ab23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20f481cb-9794-521f-abde-9e8e3e1ab23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165821} :answer-field/id #uuid "488afd3e-de10-5f8d-9291-fe35f1f5ce15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ca07102-6a61-5eae-b487-0be414d1c1ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-95983" :question/problem "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{′}=xy$\n- $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2xy=2{y}^{′}$\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-2x={y}^{″}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of third-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e2483ea-aaa8-5ec9-8ceb-1c77c24a3768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165823} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ef5d8d-d533-58b7-8279-515b42a4dcae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165824} {:answer/id #uuid "18cb3c7b-429e-50a7-9e5f-a98f4442103b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165825} {:answer/id #uuid "58305345-1cb5-54c3-b58e-fc70576c839d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165826} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac54382-1709-57fc-ac99-bbc84710cd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58305345-1cb5-54c3-b58e-fc70576c839d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165826} :answer-field/id #uuid "78bab884-1ca9-54e8-a6ce-407833d8ab94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e9b8880-609b-5a27-b50e-083098d2cbea" :question/math-academy-id "q-95984" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8af87eb9-144c-56cb-b4fe-534a251c317a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165979} {:answer/id #uuid "c498e152-a20d-5673-b5f4-1e7ea0fc2466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165980} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb27e37-f212-5440-908f-e51ae57a2c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165981} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7f7b81-ea45-53d8-be8b-6d6885443d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165982} {:answer/id #uuid "480bf9c9-efac-5f72-b748-45ed3487cd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c498e152-a20d-5673-b5f4-1e7ea0fc2466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165980} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03cdd9c-64ec-5c27-a1e9-6b636edcbc50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ca3e21f-6e14-592d-bc53-233d6a2ad52a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96387" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy={e}^{x}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′} & =xy+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2(xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2(xy+{e}^{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f0722d-0101-5178-b7dc-b2389fd00257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310209} {:answer/id #uuid "17279daa-0ab3-596b-8cbc-fa602bcff6d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310210} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa90e0-e835-5136-bb0b-818379831351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310211} {:answer/id #uuid "9057c7e4-d0dc-542f-bfc7-2bb5c4a08dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310212} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2b3e1b-d7dd-5cd3-a4d0-6dff4c777a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65f0722d-0101-5178-b7dc-b2389fd00257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310209} :answer-field/id #uuid "1274b033-3b22-555d-8137-ae27386378b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43d94d8a-fe3b-554f-ac88-a3075bf8158e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96662" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+5{y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3{y}^{5}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1752fc5a-635f-53e4-853c-360095f2087a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310215} {:answer/id #uuid "a6903af0-a20c-5e4b-99fd-253aeabbec65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310216} {:answer/id #uuid "88b9ed92-1d67-59a0-b022-096ba914be65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310217} {:answer/id #uuid "da4fee26-e1f5-5e44-872d-09cd1f79e4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310218} {:answer/id #uuid "af7b3b23-75e0-5e84-9784-9738d8098ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88b9ed92-1d67-59a0-b022-096ba914be65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310217} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ad0f14-67b0-5c3e-a518-118aa628d45b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e356353f-6fc7-5ad8-bdec-cdea5919a2a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96669" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){y}^{′}={x}^{2}y$\n- $5{y}^{″}{y}^{′}+3xy=4{y}^{′}$\n- ${({y}^{′})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=y$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "577def49-e63b-54ae-ab43-9515454a865f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310221} {:answer/id #uuid "718cd4bc-14b8-55f7-a967-0c99bef8484e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310222} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e0aabe-c67a-5b63-a7a7-2389382bcc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310223} {:answer/id #uuid "9444a130-06f2-55d6-bd72-2c8846252deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310224} {:answer/id #uuid "43edc705-7bbf-5e2f-b24b-193f37e027ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "718cd4bc-14b8-55f7-a967-0c99bef8484e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310222} :answer-field/id #uuid "86a9e1ea-f449-565b-9f12-4d37cf527e40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f8c44a4-8695-5bce-9b85-93159ef8f779" :question/math-academy-id "q-96705" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9efb605-ea2e-5276-951e-45dfc94d677e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281049} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b8be8e-1100-512a-8661-62dfd15d9af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281050} {:answer/id #uuid "b9037e85-690a-57c7-810c-89240a8faf6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281051} {:answer/id #uuid "ced67b4c-1e73-5217-a34d-7acc4b3044ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281052} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddfbb2f-742c-578c-9479-00d783882ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ced67b4c-1e73-5217-a34d-7acc4b3044ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281052} :answer-field/id #uuid "8661cba3-7048-5b7f-9533-d880c35c415e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b527fd3f-b1f2-54f1-b985-21a0b1fa81ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-96711" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "851fb5f7-f80f-53fa-a8aa-a4858b37bea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288797} {:answer/id #uuid "a5abc364-4dcd-50d2-acf2-dfcce2d06a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288798} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1e2821-38b6-54ad-a344-903cbb502127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288799} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd513d4-0551-51cc-9d9a-ae6737d7be35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288800} {:answer/id #uuid "b0dea2bc-0164-5177-ab7c-1de240772aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0dea2bc-0164-5177-ab7c-1de240772aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288801} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f54ef8c-f6d9-5953-9471-6bb05a0111cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43085691-e6df-59c5-9601-1a937f0551cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-96988" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50277299-5c67-5275-8268-3fe1a838f0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310227} {:answer/id #uuid "2f066bb8-15af-5570-a617-a58dad66e744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310228} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb3c719-1f23-5f4b-8196-fe9eef6f68bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310229} {:answer/id #uuid "974e27e8-0a1f-5427-b0df-adbff40911b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310230} {:answer/id #uuid "63062b70-3bb2-5bec-9722-dc2fdabf4989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "974e27e8-0a1f-5427-b0df-adbff40911b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310230} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b77302-cdc7-5688-860d-e49acad9f630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f324e71f-0765-5434-b640-0e2263e787be" :question/math-academy-id "q-96992" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
